    {
      "text": "Syrian President Ahmed Al-Sharaa met with Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman in Riyadh on Tuesday for an official visit, according to a report from the Saudi Press Agency. Al-Sharaa was accompanied by Syrian Foreign Minister Asaad Al-Shaibani. Earlier in the visit, Riyadh Governor Prince Faisal bin Bandar bin Abdulaziz received Al-Sharaa and Al-Shaibani upon their arrival."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "U.S. bombers withdrawn from UK air base following suspected Iranian drone plot",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The United States abruptly withdrew 12 B-1 Air Force bombers from RAF Fairford, an air base in southern England, due to information regarding a potential drone attack ordered by Iran’s Revolutionary Guard, according to U.S. and British officials. The bombers were removed over a weekend, officials said, speaking on the condition of anonymity to discuss operational matters. The suspected attack was described as part of a complex, multipronged Iranian plot intended to strike aircraft and kill some of the several hundred American personnel stationed at the base. Some officials suggested the plot may have been loosely modeled on a previous Ukrainian drone attack against Russia that utilized semitrucks to launch drones deep into Russian territory. While U.S. and British officials identified the threat, Iran has denied any involvement in the alleged plot at the base, which serves as a preferred European location for U.S. bombers. On October 6, 2026, British police reported the arrest of a 22-year-old British man in London on suspicion of preparing terrorist acts as part of their investigation into the suspected plot. A spokesperson for the U.S. military’s European Command declined to comment on specific intelligence or force protection measures, stating that U.S. bombers can deliver global strike capabilities from the continental United States."
    }
  ]
}

